    "content": "\n      <p class=\"lead-text\">Setting up a new commercial restaurant kitchen in Bengaluru's fast-growing culinary market is an exciting venture. However, equipment selection errors can lead to workflow bottlenecks, high power/gas bills, and municipal compliance hurdles. At <strong>B.K.S. Industries</strong>, having equipped over 500+ commercial kitchens across Karnataka, we have compiled the definitive 2026 kitchen equipment checklist to help restaurant owners build durable, high-efficiency kitchen operations.</p>\n\n      <h2>1. The 5 Core Commercial Kitchen Work Zones</h2>\n      <p>A well-planned commercial kitchen follows the principle of <em>linear forward workflow</em>—from raw material receiving, cold storage, preparation, cooking, plating/pickup, to dishwashing—preventing cross-contamination and kitchen collisions.</p>\n\n      <h3>Zone A: Heavy-Duty Cooking Line</h3>\n      <p>The cooking range is the engine of your restaurant. Depending on your cuisine (South Indian, North Indian, Pan-Asian, Continental, or Fast Food), you need:</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Commercial Gas Cooking Ranges:</strong> 2-Burner, 3-Burner, or 4-Burner high-pressure flame ranges fabricated in 16-gauge Heavy-Duty Commercial Grade.</li>\n        <li><strong>Chinese Jet Wok Burner:</strong> With built-in rear splash water faucet and cooling drainage channel for high-temperature wok stir-frying.</li>\n        <li><strong>Commercial Dosa Bhatti / Flat Tawa:</strong> 16mm thick polished plate for uniform heat transfer without cold spots.</li>\n        <li><strong>Commercial Deep Fryers:</strong> Dual tank (12L + 12L) thermostatic electric or gas fryers for rapid batch frying.</li>\n        <li><strong>Tilting Bratt Pan / Stockpot Stove:</strong> For high-volume curries, gravies, and biryani preparation in catering and bulk kitchens.</li>\n      </ul>\n    ",
